description:
Split a function's instructions into basic blocks and connect them, as a disassembler does before drawing a graph (lesson 2.03). Instructions are given already decoded:

```cpp
enum class Op { Plain, Call, Jump, Branch, Return };
struct Insn { std::uint32_t address; std::uint32_t length; Op op; std::uint32_t target; };
struct Block { std::uint32_t start; std::uint32_t end; std::vector<std::uint32_t> successors; };

std::vector<Block> basicBlocks(const std::vector<Insn>& code);
```

`code` is in address order with no gaps. `Jump` is an unconditional `jmp`, `Branch` a conditional jump, `Call` a `call` (its `target` is another function), `Return` a `ret`. `target` only matters for `Jump` and `Branch`.

- A block **starts** at the first instruction, at every `Jump`/`Branch` target that is one of the instructions, and at the instruction after any `Jump`, `Branch` or `Return`.
- A block's `end` is the address just past its last instruction.
- **Successors**: a block ending in `Jump` goes to its target; one ending in `Branch` goes to its target and falls through to the next instruction; one ending in `Return` has none; any other block falls through to the next block. A target outside the function (a tail call) is not a successor. Successors are sorted with no duplicates, and fall-through past the last instruction does not count.
- Blocks are returned sorted by start.

